(1214582, 150798, 5607, '<td valign="top">Nice composition, exposure and the use of a cooler light temperature really makes this shot.  I have to say though, as a person who has seen many bodies laying on morgue tables that I knew it was a fake from the minute I looked at it.  For this to have been more realistisic, there should be some blue discoloration (liver mortis) around the heels and bottoms of the legs.  That''s when the blood settles due to gravity.  This guy''s feet look alive and well. The feet also would not be hanging over the end of the table (its kind of hard to tell what type of table that is, but it doesn''t look like a morgue table to me).<br/> <br/>But keep in mind that I was a professional health care provider so I am going to look at this photo far differently from the average viewer.  Does it evoke strong emotion in me even though I see the technical flaws?  You bet.  It''s good work in my book. I think that it is worthy of a blue ribbon for many reasons and my congratualtions go to the photographer.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-03-08 09:30:54.</i></td>', 'Nice composition, exposure and the use of a cooler light temperature really makes this shot.  I have to say though, as a person who has seen many bodies laying on morgue tables that I knew it was a fake from the minute I looked at it.  For this to have been more realistisic, there should be some blue discoloration (liver mortis) around the heels and bottoms of the legs.  That''s when the blood settles due to gravity.  This guy''s feet look alive and well. The feet also would not be hanging over the end of the table (its kind of hard to tell what type of table that is, but it doesn''t look like a morgue table to me). But keep in mind that I was a professional health care provider so I am going to look at this photo far differently from the average viewer.  Does it evoke strong emotion in me even though I see the technical flaws?  You bet.  It''s good work in my book. I think that it is worthy of a blue ribbon for many reasons and my congratualtions go to the photographer.', '2005-03-08 09:29:35.000000', '2005-03-08 09:30:54.000000', 0),
(1215228, 151023, 18191, '<td valign="top">ZOOMDAK!?!?!?!?!???!?!?!!?!?! IS THAT YOU?!??!<br/> <br/>LOL sorry a little too late ;)<br/> <br/>Killer shot ---easily one of my favs in the challenge... congrats on the first ribbon!</td>', 'ZOOMDAK!?!?!?!?!???!?!?!!?!?! IS THAT YOU?!??! LOL sorry a little too late ;) Killer shot ---easily one of my favs in the challenge... congrats on the first ribbon!', '2005-03-08 14:51:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1215481, 150798, 4500, '<td valign="top">Thank you guys, it''s nice to get the blue again :)<br/> <br/>Focused: <br/>Yeah, you''re right, I know about these flaws. But it was a 48h speed challenge, and as you can imagine, it is pretty hard to find some free corpse available for shooting during the weekend. :) Thank you for your nice feedback.</td>', 'Thank you guys, it''s nice to get the blue again :) Focused: Yeah, you''re right, I know about these flaws. But it was a 48h speed challenge, and as you can imagine, it is pretty hard to find some free corpse available for shooting during the weekend. :) Thank you for your nice feedback.', '2005-03-08 17:19:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(1216131, 151038, 17507, '<td valign="top">Wow, age 14, you beat my brother who started at 15. For a 50-year smoker at age 67, based on your self portrait, you''re in really good shape! I always love seeing an exception to the rule :)</td>', 'Wow, age 14, you beat my brother who started at 15. For a 50-year smoker at age 67, based on your self portrait, you''re in really good shape! I always love seeing an exception to the rule :)', '2005-03-09 00:06:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(1217312, 150798, 5607, '<td valign="top">LOL! yeah, unless you have access to your local hospital or county morgue it''s pretty hard to come up with a dead body in 48 hours. . . . but you could create the liver mortis with make-up, although that gets a bit messy. . . . in any event, good job!</td>', 'LOL! yeah, unless you have access to your local hospital or county morgue it''s pretty hard to come up with a dead body in 48 hours. . . . but you could create the liver mortis with make-up, although that gets a bit messy. . . . in any event, good job!', '2005-03-09 10:23:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1217632, 150798, 33467, '<td valign="top">My opinion: Poorly composed, toe tag very unecessary, too dark....sorry, can''t all give you kudos all the time!</td>', 'My opinion: Poorly composed, toe tag very unecessary, too dark....sorry, can''t all give you kudos all the time!', '2005-03-09 13:03:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(1217903, 150967, 34753, '<td valign="top">Very versatile for use as a billboard.</td>', 'Very versatile for use as a billboard.', '2005-03-09 14:32:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1217971, 150565, 31637, '<td valign="top">Hello Gina. This photo was given to me to critique. I understand it was for a billboard challenge, and you did not meet the requirements for the theme. You were supposed to scale the image a certain way and make it seem billboard shaped. The photo itself is not bad. It is clear, good focus on the subject. The Flash? I think you were too close to her to use the flash. In this case you did not overexpose her face, but nextime try to use other methods of lighting for self portraits like this. Do you have spotlights? or any highhat lighting in your house. Try to experiment by directing light onto the subject for different looks.  <br/> <br/>good luck...Anthony</td>', 'Hello Gina. This photo was given to me to critique. I understand it was for a billboard challenge, and you did not meet the requirements for the theme. You were supposed to scale the image a certain way and make it seem billboard shaped. The photo itself is not bad. It is clear, good focus on the subject. The Flash? I think you were too close to her to use the flash. In this case you did not overexpose her face, but nextime try to use other methods of lighting for self portraits like this. Do you have spotlights? or any highhat lighting in your house. Try to experiment by directing light onto the subject for different looks.   good luck...Anthony', '2005-03-09 14:56:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(1218023, 150404, 31637, '<td valign="top">Hi Cherl. I liked this photo, its a good billboard and your tank really fills up the frame. Its  a good crop, you have nice sharp focus and a nice contrast. You used an ISO of 1600 and a shutter @ 1/4000 of a sec. Thats a really fast shutter and tanks dont move at high speeds so you could of gotten away with a 1/1000 shutter speed for this shot.  Your photo is grainless and considering your high ISO, you have a really neat photo here.  Your background could of been a little more interesting if there were no trees and just sky. However, you just cant move trees out of the way so you did a good job achieving good DOF (depth of field) and maintained strong focus throughout. When someone looks at such an amazing tool or vehicle such as this tank, it gets your blood pumping and would definatly motivate people to join the army, keep up the good work<br/> <br/>-Anthony-</td>', 'Hi Cherl. I liked this photo, its a good billboard and your tank really fills up the frame. Its  a good crop, you have nice sharp focus and a nice contrast. You used an ISO of 1600 and a shutter @ 1/4000 of a sec. Thats a really fast shutter and tanks dont move at high speeds so you could of gotten away with a 1/1000 shutter speed for this shot.  Your photo is grainless and considering your high ISO, you have a really neat photo here.  Your background could of been a little more interesting if there were no trees and just sky. However, you just cant move trees out of the way so you did a good job achieving good DOF (depth of field) and maintained strong focus throughout. When someone looks at such an amazing tool or vehicle such as this tank, it gets your blood pumping and would definatly motivate people to join the army, keep up the good work -Anthony-', '2005-03-09 15:13:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(1218414, 150703, 14773, '<td valign="top">I found some of the comments here interesting. But some people seemed to miss that all advertising is geared for a specific audience - not to everyone.  A UCOB ad is most definetly not geared towards middle aged men. Sorry guys! That being said, it''s a great reason why a lot of people weren''t "sold" (pun intended) on this image deserving a 10.<br/>It''s a fantastic shot, and being a 20-something female (ie their target audience) this shot would probably get my attention and maybe even get me to buy that scarf :-)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-03-09 18:24:22.</i></td>', 'I found some of the comments here interesting. But some people seemed to miss that all advertising is geared for a specific audience - not to everyone.  A UCOB ad is most definetly not geared towards middle aged men. Sorry guys! That being said, it''s a great reason why a lot of people weren''t "sold" (pun intended) on this image deserving a 10.It''s a fantastic shot, and being a 20-something female (ie their target audience) this shot would probably get my attention and maybe even get me to buy that scarf :-)', '2005-03-09 18:23:45.000000', '2005-03-09 18:24:22.000000', 0),
(1218595, 150404, 31637, '<td valign="top">By the way Cherl, your photo resolution should be just under 150kb. Try not to compress your submissions, you will lose quality.</td>', 'By the way Cherl, your photo resolution should be just under 150kb. Try not to compress your submissions, you will lose quality.', '2005-03-09 19:58:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1218702, 150615, 31637, '<td valign="top">This photo is real nice. I love wine and it''s nice to see such compositions. Your 2 subjects are in perfect positions. The Woman and the field. your DOF is ok, and lighting too. However..for a billboard you need some type of photo that grabs and demands attention. Im not sure if this composition pulls it off. I do like the fact how her glass is empty, giving the impression that she is waiting for the grapes to grow.</td>', 'This photo is real nice. I love wine and it''s nice to see such compositions. Your 2 subjects are in perfect positions. The Woman and the field. your DOF is ok, and lighting too. However..for a billboard you need some type of photo that grabs and demands attention. Im not sure if this composition pulls it off. I do like the fact how her glass is empty, giving the impression that she is waiting for the grapes to grow.', '2005-03-09 21:03:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1219067, 151023, 33337, '<td valign="top">i live in Cannon Beach by zoomdak. it looks like a zoomdak but without the haystack rock. I love it. It is something new.</td>', 'i live in Cannon Beach by zoomdak. it looks like a zoomdak but without the haystack rock. I love it. It is something new.', '2005-03-10 01:06:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(1219443, 151023, 20889, '<td valign="top">Great shot Michael, love the colors.</td>', 'Great shot Michael, love the colors.', '2005-03-10 09:42:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(1221062, 150439, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club.  It''s been quite awhile since I pulled up a photo to critique.  If I keep getting ones like this, I''ll have to do it more often :)<br/> <br/>I really like the composition and the way your subject seems to pop out from the rest of the photo.  Catching him in the middle of a song, playing and singing, makes the subject stronger than if he was just sitting with the guitar.  I almost feel like the viewer is his audience.<br/> <br/>The background does seem a bit busy for the challenge, but not for the photo as it stands alone.  The colors and activity in the background give a great feel of putting the subject in context.  The background is bright, yes - but details are still visable, even at the end of the street.  I think it adds some liveliness to the shot.  <br/> <br/>I have a great pair of gloves like that for playing cold-weather guitar also (great for winter photography as well!)<br/> <br/>Overall, an excellent job of a candid, photojournalistic photo.  I don''t think I''d change a thing.  <br/> <br/>Hope this is helpful!  <br/>Judy<br/></td>', 'Hello from the critique club.  It''s been quite awhile since I pulled up a photo to critique.  If I keep getting ones like this, I''ll have to do it more often :) I really like the composition and the way your subject seems to pop out from the rest of the photo.  Catching him in the middle of a song, playing and singing, makes the subject stronger than if he was just sitting with the guitar.  I almost feel like the viewer is his audience. The background does seem a bit busy for the challenge, but not for the photo as it stands alone.  The colors and activity in the background give a great feel of putting the subject in context.  The background is bright, yes - but details are still visable, even at the end of the street.  I think it adds some liveliness to the shot.   I have a great pair of gloves like that for playing cold-weather guitar also (great for winter photography as well!) Overall, an excellent job of a candid, photojournalistic photo.  I don''t think I''d change a thing.   Hope this is helpful!  Judy', '2005-03-11 00:06:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1221064, 150798, 5607, '<td valign="top">Um, don''t know the rules about jumping into a comment section about one of the winning shots, but I wanted to ask Nawets about his monitor calibration.  I''m looking at this with an Apple 30" HD Cinema Display that is well-calibrated and the photo is not at all dark.  Is your contrast adjusted correctly?<br/> <br/>Also, just wanted to add that I think the toe tag is necessary in the shot if you want to give context for a MVA due to speed.  The tag gives a very impersonal, almost "statistical" quality to this photo that gives it impact in my view.  As a former healthcare person who has seen thousands of dead human remains (yes, thousands), this struck me because after a while, you start to just categorize them in certain ways -- such as "the MVA, the CVA, the MI, the Suicide, the homicide," etc. . . .  In this photo, the photographer chose to illustrate a speed MVA fatality with that one item that grabs people''s attention -- a toe tag.  In fact, how would one even really know that the person is dead without it?  This could be a tall guy waiting for a massage and treatment at the physical therapists office for all we know. . . .  They use white sheets.  And in this day and age, I haven''t seen a dead body in a clinical setting without a toe tag -- too much risk of a mix-up.  so yes, the toe tag works in my view. . . .</td>', 'Um, don''t know the rules about jumping into a comment section about one of the winning shots, but I wanted to ask Nawets about his monitor calibration.  I''m looking at this with an Apple 30" HD Cinema Display that is well-calibrated and the photo is not at all dark.  Is your contrast adjusted correctly? Also, just wanted to add that I think the toe tag is necessary in the shot if you want to give context for a MVA due to speed.  The tag gives a very impersonal, almost "statistical" quality to this photo that gives it impact in my view.  As a former healthcare person who has seen thousands of dead human remains (yes, thousands), this struck me because after a while, you start to just categorize them in certain ways -- such as "the MVA, the CVA, the MI, the Suicide, the homicide," etc. . . .  In this photo, the photographer chose to illustrate a speed MVA fatality with that one item that grabs people''s attention -- a toe tag.  In fact, how would one even really know that the person is dead without it?  This could be a tall guy waiting for a massage and treatment at the physical therapists office for all we know. . . .  They use white sheets.  And in this day and age, I haven''t seen a dead body in a clinical setting without a toe tag -- too much risk of a mix-up.  so yes, the toe tag works in my view. . . .', '2005-03-11 00:09:59.000000', NULL, 0),
